      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hersey was officially platted on July 9 1867 by banker and lumber baron Blodgett, who named it after early Muskegon River trapper Nathan Hersey.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hersey_michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Blodgett donated lumber for the late\u20111860s Evangelical Association church and funded the Hersey Congregational Church building in 1873\u201174.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hersey_michigan",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Hersey River empties into the Muskegon River at the village\u2019s edge, creating a notable confluence that shapes the local landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hersey_michigan",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "When the Flint & Pere Marquette Railroad arrived in 1871, Hersey became a vital stop for transporting logs and finished boards.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hersey_michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "As a lumber town, Hersey supplied the Muskegon Lumber Yards and helped rebuild Chicago after the great fire.",
